  Before he unshackled the tow, he climbed aboard the [3mBluebell[0m one last time, unbolted the green brass bell from her foredeck, and carried it back to
  the [3mHester[0m in both arms.

  It hangs now on a post at the end of his garden, facing the water. On weekday mornings at 7:20, the neighbors say, you can hear it ring: two short,
  one long.

[38;5;246m✻[39m [38;5;246mCrunched for 36s · done 11:49 PM[39m

[38;5;246m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mWithout using any tools, write a 300-word poem about fog.[39m
[49m
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Captain, here's the poem:

  [1mWhat the Fog Keeps[0m

  It comes in before the tide does,
  quiet as a cat on a windowsill,
  and lays its grey wool over the harbor
  until the boats forget their names.

  The lighthouse becomes a rumor.
  The far shore turns into a theory.
  Even the gulls go hushed and careful,
  calling out to check that the world is still there.

  I walk the seawall with my hands in my pockets
  and the town folds up behind me
  like a letter put away for later.
  Ten steps, and the bakery is gone.
  Twenty, and I can't see my own front door,
  only the yellow smear of a porch light
  somebody left on, just in case.

  Fog is not darkness.
  Darkness takes everything at once.
  Fog is slower and more polite.
  It borrows the world one piece at a time
  and promises, mostly, to give it back.


